@page "/mechanism/2-es-modules"

<PageTitle>2. ES module imports — JS interop demos</PageTitle>

<div class="mechanism-page">
    <MechanismNav />

    <header class="mech-head">
        <p class="mech-kicker">
            Mechanism 2 of @Mechanisms.All.Count · you are reading this in
            <span class="host-chip">@RuntimeMode.Name</span> — the same page in
            <CounterpartLink />
        </p>
        <h1>ES module imports: the modern replacement</h1>
        <p class="mech-lede">
            Import a module once, keep the reference, call its exports through it. Scoped instead of
            global, composable, and the module keeps private state between calls. This is the
            production default on both hosting models.
        </p>
        <p class="demo-support">
            Available in: <strong>@Mechanisms.Availability(2)</strong>
        </p>
    </header>

    <section class="mech-block">
        <h2>The boundary you cannot skip</h2>
        <p>
            A module's exports are reachable <strong>only</strong> through the module reference. The
            root <code>IJSRuntime</code> from mechanism 1 still resolves names against the global
            scope, and an ES module never puts anything there. So importing the module and then
            calling <code>JS.InvokeAsync&lt;string&gt;("getMessage")</code> on the root runtime fails
            even though the export exists and the module is loaded.
        </p>
        <p>
            The reference is a tracked object, and it is <em>you</em> who releases it: dispose the
            module when the component goes away, or the handle outlives the component that needed it.
            <a href="@Mechanisms.Href(4)">Mechanism 4</a> shows what that looks like
            from the other side.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mech-block">
        <h2>What the panels print in @RuntimeMode.Name</h2>
        <p class="mech-out-label">✓ Call an export through the module reference</p>
        <pre class="mech-out">Hello from the ES module — call #1. The counter survived the previous call.</pre>

        <p class="mech-out-label">✗ Call that same export through the root runtime</p>
        <pre class="mech-out mech-out-fail">JSException: The value 'getMessage' is not a function.
Error: The value 'getMessage' is not a function.</pre>

        <p class="mech-note">
            The <em>call number</em> in the first result is module-private state: it increments across
            panels and across runs, which is the practical difference from a global. The failure is
            identical on both hosts, and identical in wording to mechanism 1's typo — the runtime
            cannot tell you that the function exists, just not where you looked for it.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mech-block">
        <h2>Paths, and the subdirectory that breaks them</h2>
        <p>
            The asset path is relative to the app's base URI, so it is written
            <code>./_content/DemoInterop.Shared/js/module.js</code>. A rooted path
            (<code>/_content/…</code>) resolves against the <em>site</em> root instead, and under a
            GitHub Pages project site that silently drops the <code>/demo-blazor-interop/</code>
            segment: 404. The same trap is what <a href="@Mechanisms.Href(6)">mechanism 6</a> builds
            its URL from <code>Navigation.BaseUri</code> rather than a literal.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mech-split">
        <div class="mech-use">
            <h2>Use it when</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>You call the same module's functions repeatedly.</li>
                <li>You want scoped JavaScript that can hold private state between calls.</li>
                <li>You want one interop surface that behaves the same on Server and WebAssembly.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="mech-never">
            <h2>Never when</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>You want the compiler to check argument types. That is
                    <a href="@Mechanisms.Href(6)">mechanism 6</a>, and it is WebAssembly-only.</li>
                <li>You are willing to leak the reference. An undisposed module handle is a
                    permanent entry in the runtime's tracked-object table.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

    <h2 class="mech-live-head">The live panels</h2>
    <CascadingValue Name="MechanismPageOwnsHeading" Value="true">
        <Demo02Modules />
    </CascadingValue>

    <MechanismPager Number="2" />
</div>
